      "writtenOn": "2020-11-18T07:59:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "the lack of check was decided on purpose very long time ago (\u003e 6 years) because this is a really critical path. Instead there was the ASSERT() above protected by CONFIG_DEBUG_BRINGUP to ensure no incorrect usage where slipping",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b71a8af5_ca9386b2",
        "filename": "core/cortex-m/task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-18T18:03:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can see an argument for keeping this path as speedy as possible, but at the same time if another caller uses this incorrectly then we can get into a deeply undesirable state of just bootlooping the EC until battery drains.  We\u0027d need to be very confident that our FW testing has caught all possibilities of this happening before any release without a check like this.",
      "parentUuid": "b306fc8a_f9e41ea0",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d84b1ce_05fb82d0",
        "filename": "core/cortex-m/task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-19T00:27:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that the cost of this check seems well worth accepting given the cost of error (as that\u0027s kind of the unstated premise of the change). Most developers don\u0027t work with CONFIG_DEBUG_BRINGUP enabled so the existing guard will often fail to catch misuse, and uncaught misuse will not always be obvious (as in this case where failures only occur when we get unlucky with placement of values in RAM).\n\nLooking at it more critically, I believe the status quo is actually Undefined Behavior: pointer arithmetic between scratchpad and tasks is undefined because they do not refer to members of the same array (C99 6.5.6.8). There is no compiler option in use to make this defined (nor do I believe one exists), so I\u0027d say this counts as a bug even though no compiler currently seems to have a problem with it.\n\nConcretely, this change increases the happy path on cortex-m from 9 instructions to 12, and I believe this could be reduced to 10 or 11 by making some changes to how scheduler data is declared in order to improve memory locality if it were important. The most critical user of this function (based on a quick scan) appears to be the scheduler itself or maybe mutex functions, which I believe are not so critical as to warrant retaining a bug in exchange for a few tens of nanoseconds of CPU time per invocation.",
      "parentUuid": "b71a8af5_ca9386b2",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c9c49fe1_56e15208",
        "filename": "core/cortex-m/task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-19T17:47:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One way to improve the situation is to separate task_get_current to _task_get_current (internal \u0026 fast) and task_get_current (external \u0026 safe).\n\nFor example, all TCPM code can call the safe version because they don\u0027t care about 3 instructions. Some task APIs such as task_disable_task can also call the safe version for the same reason.\n\nsvc_handler has already its own:\n\n\t\t/*\n\t\t * Remove our own ready bit (current - tasks is same as\n\t\t * task_get_current())\n\t\t */\n\t\ttasks_ready \u0026\u003d ~(1 \u003c\u003c (current - tasks));\n\nprobably because it knows it\u0027s in a critical path.",
      "parentUuid": "5d84b1ce_05fb82d0",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fdc4ccc_1e6cf8a4",
        "filename": "core/cortex-m/task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-19T18:03:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That may not have fixed the case we hit here though, right?  Since EFS2 was calling in through task_disable_task() which would use the internal version?  Or would all externally usable functions in this be made safe, and then anything calling internally should already be checked? \n\nI keep wondering if we could set up a unit test to verify the EC main thread doesn\u0027t call into these task functions before task_start? That could provide us enough testing safety to keep the call speedy, assuming we can get sufficient coverage of all the different CONFIG options that can happen during EC startup.",
      "parentUuid": "c9c49fe1_56e15208",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2281df6a_c5a1f655",
        "filename": "core/cortex-m/task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 253,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-20T00:58:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks to me like with svc_handler already doing the equivalent of calling an unsafe variant there\u0027s no significant need to actually create one. I spotted a few usages that ensure they\u0027re run in a task context by other means which could be converted but don\u0027t seem performance-critical, and the other usages seem like they aren\u0027t both verifiably safe and performance-critical.\n\nPerformance tuning beyond the immediate users of task_get_task would need to expose unsafe variants of related functions such as mutex_lock, which seems like a lot of complexity for small (possibly irrelevant) benefit.\n\nTesting sounds like it would be fragile, since it would be easy to add config options that wouldn\u0027t be covered and could reintroduce errors in some configurations.",
      "parentUuid": "4fdc4ccc_1e6cf8a4",
      "revId": "87a572c3c44210cd36d14e3433b196d153221ec2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
